package docs

import (
	"github.com/filipejohansson/vane/core"
)

func LanguageSpec() core.Node {
	core.Head(func() core.HeadConfig {
		title := ".vane Language Spec | Vane Docs"
		description := "The syntax .vane files must follow, and what's covered by Vane's compatibility contract."
		url := core.Window().Get("location").Get("origin").String() + "/vane/docs/language-spec"
		return core.HeadConfig{
			Title:         title,
			Description:   description,
			Canonical:     url,
			OGTitle:       title,
			OGDescription: description,
			OGURL:         url,
		}
	})

	items := languageSpecTOCItems

	return (
		<div className="docs-page">
			<article className="docs-article">
				{docsHeader(
					"Reference",
					".vane Language Spec",
					"The syntax .vane files must follow to compile.",
				)}

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="scope">What this page covers</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					This is a reference for the syntax the <code>.vane</code> compiler accepts:
					what compiles, what doesn't, and how ambiguous cases resolve. Existing valid{" "}
					<code>.vane</code> files keep compiling across future minor and patch releases,
					so anything documented here can be relied on to keep working.{" "}
					{apiDocLink("concepts", "Concepts")}, {apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")},
					and {apiDocLink("components", "Components")} stay the place to learn the
					language; this page is the place to check the exact rule.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="elements">Elements</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					A lowercase tag name compiles to an HTML element, e.g. <code>{"<div>"}</code>{" "}
					compiles to <code>core.El("div")</code>. Self-closing (<code>{"<br/>"}</code>)
					and explicitly closed (<code>{"<div></div>"}</code>) forms are both accepted;
					void elements such as <code>{"<input/>"}</code> don't require children either
					way. The full static/reactive prop and event tables live on{" "}
					{apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")}.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="components">Components</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					A tag whose name starts with an uppercase letter is <strong>not</strong> valid
					vane syntax — components are plain Go function calls, not tags.{" "}
					<code>{"<Counter/>"}</code> fails to compile with a "not valid vane syntax"
					error, hinting the fix: call it as an expression, <code>{"{Counter()}"}</code>.
					This is the language's actual element-vs-component ambiguity rule: the compiler
					decides purely from the first letter's case, mirroring Go's own
					exported/unexported convention. See {apiDocLink("components", "Components")} for
					children, <code>HTMLProps</code>, and the controller/view pattern built on top of
					this rule.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="fragments">Fragments</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					<code>{"<></>"}</code> groups multiple nodes without a wrapper element, at the
					root of a <code>return</code> or nested inside another element. A fragment
					nested inside an element appends its children directly to that parent — no
					extra wrapper node is emitted for it.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="text-and-comments">Text and comments</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					Plain text inside an element becomes a text node. A <code>{"//"}</code> or{" "}
					<code>{"/*"}</code> is only treated as a comment marker when nothing but
					whitespace comes before it in that run of text — right after the previous tag,
					or at the very start of an element's children. If real text already precedes
					it, it's left alone:
				</p>
				<ul className="docs-list">
					<li><code>{"http://localhost:8080"}</code> — "http:" comes before the{" "}
						<code>{"//"}</code>, so it's just text.</li>
					<li><code>{"Block comments start with /* and end with */"}</code> — words come
						before the <code>{"/*"}</code>, so it's just text too.</li>
				</ul>
				<p className="docs-p">
					When a <code>{"//"}</code> is recognized as a comment, everything to the end of
					that physical source line is dropped — including any closing tag that happens
					to share the line. Writing an element whose entire content is <code>{"//"}</code>{" "}
					drops that element's own closing tag along with it. Wrap it in a string
					expression instead — <code>{"{\"//\"}"}</code> — to render it as literal text.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="expressions-and-spreads">Expressions and spreads</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					<code>{"{expr}"}</code> embeds a single reactive child node. <code>{"{slice...}"}</code>{" "}
					spread compiles to one of two different things depending on what's spread:
				</p>
				<ul className="docs-list">
					<li>Written as a function call, e.g. <code>{"{items()...}"}</code>, it compiles
						to <code>core.DynList</code> — a reactive, optionally keyed list binding that
						only touches the DOM nodes that actually changed.</li>
					<li>Written as a plain value, e.g. a variadic children parameter or an existing{" "}
						<code>[]core.Node</code> spread as <code>{"{children...}"}</code>, it compiles
						to <code>core.Fragment(core.Spread(children)...)</code> instead — a plain
						one-time spread, not a tracked <code>DynList</code>.</li>
				</ul>
				<p className="docs-p">
					See {apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")}'s "Reactive lists" section for the
					full walkthrough, including <code>key</code>.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="attributes-and-props">Attributes and props</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					A static string prop (<code>{"className=\"card\""}</code>) is set once; a
					<code>{"{expr}"}</code> prop is reactive and re-applies when a signal it reads
					changes. A bare boolean attribute, e.g. <code>{"<input disabled/>"}</code>, is
					treated as an implicit <code>{"{true}"}</code> expression. <code>{"key={expr}"}</code>{" "}
					and <code>{"ref={...}"}</code> are compiler-recognized special props, not passed
					through as DOM attributes. The full prop/event name table is on{" "}
					{apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")}; how those HTML/JSX-style names map onto
					real DOM properties (and the <code>Extra</code> escape hatch for names with no
					dedicated field) is on {apiDocLink("components", "Components")}'s{" "}
					<code>HTMLProps</code> section.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="control-flow">Inline control flow</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					<code>{"{for ...}"}</code>, <code>{"{if ...}"}</code>, and{" "}
					<code>{"{switch ...}"}</code> may appear directly as JSX children, each compiled
					to a reactive binding that reruns only when a signal it reads changes. Full
					syntax and constraints (one JSX element per branch or iteration, wrapping
					multiple nodes in a fragment, <code>key</code> for keyed loops) are covered on{" "}
					{apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")} rather than restated here.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="where-it-compiles">Where .vane syntax compiles</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					JSX only compiles inside a <code>return (...)</code> statement. Assigning it to
					a variable, or leaving a <code>func() core.Node {"{ ... }"}</code> literal
					uncalled, are both compile errors — the exact patterns and their fixes are
					covered on {apiDocLink("jsx-syntax", "JSX Syntax")}'s "JSX rules" section.
				</p>

				<h2 className="docs-h2" id="compiler-errors">Compiler errors</h2>
				<p className="docs-p">
					Invalid syntax — an unclosed tag, a wrong closing tag, an unknown event
					attribute, an uppercase component tag used as an element, and more — fails at
					compile time with the file, line, and column, a snippet of source with a caret,
					and, where the mistake maps to a likely correction, a "did you mean" hint. If a{" "}
					<code>.vane</code> file doesn't compile, the error itself says what's wrong and
					where — there's nothing here to separately memorize.
				</p>

				{pageNav("language-spec")}
			</article>
			{toc(items)}
		</div>
	)
}
